#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc2efb6-9eca-5951-8e7b-36ca586cc91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465663} {:answer/id #uuid "4c94335e-da4f-5437-a16e-06f74b8263ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c94335e-da4f-5437-a16e-06f74b8263ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465664} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa48675-e440-5d05-bce2-e8ba5671377c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466675} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebb7ea2d-fda8-5bb3-8b30-53e628e49462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465665} {:answer/id #uuid "4a519b06-c877-5f24-b5ab-94c362d829af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a519b06-c877-5f24-b5ab-94c362d829af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465666} :answer-field/id #uuid "b36b6346-2bec-52b5-be58-2c49b45e65b8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67109f8f-67eb-5347-861b-978220010be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465667} {:answer/id #uuid "3f25123c-227b-583e-a1b9-25f891486d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f25123c-227b-583e-a1b9-25f891486d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465668} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a91194-5a35-55f8-b752-19469120d84b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9ed15ab-e314-5efe-ab81-8f51e9de96ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-178026" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{12}{25}+\\frac{12}{25}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}+(\\frac{3}{5})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{3}{5})^{2}+(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d3fe7ca-ea29-5f3f-8066-d8716cb13a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525269} {:answer/id #uuid "32717fe3-a7f3-56f5-9f8f-5f4636b81c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525330} {:answer/id #uuid "4d91a2bf-fdbf-56b8-8e4e-20e5996d747e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525401} {:answer/id #uuid "80bb61ff-4612-53a2-9ac0-e8606aaa9455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525527} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef395c5-e379-5f31-abfb-14bad75b8656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d3fe7ca-ea29-5f3f-8066-d8716cb13a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525269} :answer-field/id #uuid "7be66281-82ba-5671-8686-d76669d37ebe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01fe6b4b-89e7-5f7d-85f4-05ba3856f335" :question/math-academy-id "q-178268" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2+2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1f6862c-27fd-5c39-8eb2-3cbbffed6df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465669} {:answer/id #uuid "786a7ab2-a3ec-5b80-8a00-e9e1753b87d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1f6862c-27fd-5c39-8eb2-3cbbffed6df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465669} :answer-field/id #uuid "55fa8074-b199-52f0-b9ec-a64e6d2e74dd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cbd69a-f982-5d8b-a7b5-faa1d54ef043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465671} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dbd501-b3c0-564f-92ea-a9a572fb9e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14cbd69a-f982-5d8b-a7b5-faa1d54ef043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465671} :answer-field/id #uuid "77082866-697e-5473-bb08-593811756def" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270b69ea-ae43-51a2-bc71-ac5760e7b855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465673} {:answer/id #uuid "f89e42f6-9f2a-5d60-9603-c8642f276749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "270b69ea-ae43-51a2-bc71-ac5760e7b855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465673} :answer-field/id #uuid "07d18537-0a6d-59ae-9629-b8bdfac0f081" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e16f6fa7-ae80-51bb-bdf3-1add916dcb13" :question/math-academy-id "q-178269" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=6+3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5739013-0ec3-5054-9df2-0416169aee06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197739} {:answer/id #uuid "cb80e9c9-9a83-5c04-a3ab-b7735d5f125a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197740} {:answer/id #uuid "cc77a078-56f6-5c64-a1d0-37853dbff1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197741} {:answer/id #uuid "e099e132-6017-54bd-af9b-dabda71bcfb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197742} {:answer/id #uuid "2c36f56b-9a3f-5112-a631-00ca6bd5658b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e099e132-6017-54bd-af9b-dabda71bcfb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197742} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec57d5bb-fbdf-5711-9938-796ddd4e592a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b4e4425-10cb-5811-9da8-6b08b3f0fd92" :question/math-academy-id "q-40489" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e1aa1e-de86-5141-a747-49b7df88362b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324305} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb428da-53ea-533b-ad36-0e999294e68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324318} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee62035-a9e5-5f7f-9961-5d46b2a9cb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324345} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1e464f-a9e3-5cb7-9c86-438e31176d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324478} {:answer/id #uuid "abecbcfc-c2c9-55b7-a6bd-b251b89a9b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f1e464f-a9e3-5cb7-9c86-438e31176d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324478} :answer-field/id #uuid "521acea1-0e27-577c-b181-7478a17ba862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "063b2ff3-4387-5a0a-88b2-8a1c68641ed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40516" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
